.TH FSCK.BEAMFS 8 "2026-08-27" "beamfs 1.5.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
fsck.beamfs \- check and repair a beamfs filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsck.beamfs
.RI [ options ]
.I device
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsck.beamfs
checks the structural integrity of an unmounted beamfs filesystem and,
when asked, repairs what it can.
.PP
beamfs corrects single\-event and multi\-bit upsets online, inside the
kernel, on every read. This tool is the offline complement, for the
cases the kernel cannot or should not handle: after a mount has been
refused, before trusting a volume that has been stored or transported,
or as a full inventory after a known electromagnetic event. It operates
on the block device directly and never runs against a mounted
filesystem.
.PP
Five passes run in order. Each depends on the previous one having
established trustworthy on\-disk state.
.TP
.B Pass 1 \- superblock
Reads block 0, RS\-decodes the thirteen shortened RS(255,239)
subblocks covering the superblock, then verifies
.IR s_crc32 .
The magic number lives inside the RS\-covered region, so a corrupted
magic is a candidate for correction rather than an immediate abort.
.I s_crc32
itself is deliberately outside RS coverage: a flip there can only
produce a false mismatch against data RS has already proven correct,
so a clean RS decode with a mismatching CRC is treated as a stale
checksum and, under
.BR \-\-repair ,
restated from the computed value.
.IP
If this pass finds the superblock untrustworthy \- bad magic without
.BR \-\-force ,
or RS\-uncorrectable content \- passes 2 to 5 are skipped rather than
run against block offsets derived from unvalidated fields.
.TP
.B Pass 2 \- block bitmap
RS\-decodes each of the sixteen subblocks in every bitmap block. A
subblock RS cannot correct means the roughly 15000 allocation bits it
carries are untrustworthy in either direction; recovering from that is
pass 4's job, not this one's.
.TP
.B Pass 3 \- inode table
For every occupied inode, verifies CRC32 first and attempts RS
correction only on mismatch, re\-verifying the CRC afterwards \- the
same order the kernel uses on the read path. Direct pointers and the
single, double and triple indirect trees are then walked and every
pointer bounds\-checked, and checked against the allocation bitmap.
.IP
Indirection blocks carry no checksum of their own: 512 pointers occupy
a full 4096\-byte block with no room for one. A corrupted pointer that
lands on another allocated block in range is therefore
indistinguishable from a legitimate one, here as in the kernel. See
.B Documentation/data\-protection\-design.md
section 6.1.
.TP
.B Pass 4 \- bitmap rebuild
Builds a reference bitmap from every pointer reachable from the inode
table and compares it against the on\-disk bitmap. Two divergences are
reported: a block referenced by an inode but marked free, which risks
silent data loss if that block is handed out again, and a block marked
used but referenced by nothing, which is a leak. Under
.BR \-\-repair ,
the on\-disk bitmap is rewritten to match the inode table.
.TP
.B Pass 5 \- RS event journal
Validates the 64\-entry journal: per\-entry CRC32, the two structural
sentinel fields that must stay zero, and timestamp monotonicity in
write order from the circular head. The journal is a forensic log, not
redo state, so nothing is replayed. Under
.BR \-\-repair ,
an entry failing its own CRC32 is zeroed rather than guessed at; the
superblock's CRC and RS parity are then re\-derived, since the journal
lives inside the superblock's RS\-covered region.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-check\-only
Report only, modify nothing. This is the default. A correctable error
found in this mode is reported as
.BR 4 ,
not
.BR 0 :
a correction computed in memory and hold pointers and no parity describes them back did not happen
to the volume.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-repair
Write corrections back to the device.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Continue past sanity checks that would otherwise abort early, such as a
superblock whose magic number is wrong after RS correction. Intended
for forensic recovery where the operator accepts the risk of operating
on a volume that may not be beamfs at all.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print per\-pass progress to standard output. Errors go to standard
error regardless.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit codes follow the
.BR fsck (8)
convention.
.TP
.B 0
No errors.
.TP
.B 1
Errors were found and corrected. Only possible under
.BR \-\-repair .
.TP
.B 4
Errors were found and left uncorrected. Either
.B \-\-check\-only
was in effect, or the damage exceeded what RS and the bitmap rebuild
can recover.
.TP
.B 8
Operational error: the device could not be opened or read.
.TP
.B 16
Usage or syntax error.
.TP
.B 32
Canceled by the operator.
.TP
.B 128
Shared library error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a volume without touching it:
.PP
.RS 4
.B fsck.beamfs \-n /dev/vdb
.RE
.PP
Repair, with per\-pass progress:
.PP
.RS 4
.B fsck.beamfs \-\-repair \-\-verbose /dev/vdb
.RE
.PP
Attempt recovery on a volume whose superblock magic is wrong:
.PP
.RS 4
.B fsck.beamfs \-\-repair \-\-force /dev/vdb
.RE
.SH NOTES
The Reed\-Solomon decoder is a userland port of
.B lib/reed_solomon/
from the Linux kernel tree, so its correction behaviour matches the
in\-kernel decoder byte for byte. The encoder used when rewriting
parity is verified against the same reference.
.PP
This tool does not replace online RS correction, does not run as a
daemon, does not repair a mounted filesystem, does not perform
cryptographic verification, and does not replay a metadata journal \-
beamfs has none. See
.B Documentation/fsck.beamfs.md
section 2.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsck (8),
.BR mkfs.beamfs (8),
.BR e2fsck (8)
.PP
.B Documentation/fsck.beamfs.md
in the beamfs source tree, for the full scope and design rationale.
